3
00:00:15,040 --> 00:00:22,410
This is a topic that I find
fascinating for some reason.

4
00:00:22,410 --> 00:00:24,940
These routers, the actual

5
00:00:24,940 --> 00:00:29,320
devices themselves are
taking for granted.

6
00:00:29,320 --> 00:00:32,790
We do all networking all day,

7
00:00:32,790 --> 00:00:37,540
and we take for granted
the network just

8
00:00:37,540 --> 00:00:39,680
works a lot of times
and it's due to

9
00:00:39,680 --> 00:00:42,660
the routers in the
design of them.

10
00:00:42,660 --> 00:00:44,580
It's really fascinating.
There's a lot of

11
00:00:44,580 --> 00:00:46,520
innovation that went into it.

12
00:00:46,520 --> 00:00:51,500
We're going to take a quick
peek at what's inside,

13
00:00:51,500 --> 00:00:54,340
how these routers are designed,

14
00:00:54,340 --> 00:01:00,130
and then go from there.

15
00:01:00,130 --> 00:01:02,895
We're calling it a router.

16
00:01:02,895 --> 00:01:05,300
What is the role of a router?

17
00:01:05,300 --> 00:01:08,000
The first main role

18
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:11,180
is we talked about this
gateway last time.

19
00:01:11,180 --> 00:01:14,540
The gateway when we
have a land which

20
00:01:14,540 --> 00:01:17,380
has a link layer
technology that needs

21
00:01:17,380 --> 00:01:20,500
to connect to a
different network

22
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:23,440
that has a different
link layer technology,

23
00:01:23,440 --> 00:01:27,175
different administrative
domain, whatever it is.

24
00:01:27,175 --> 00:01:30,540
The gateway is that
border that interface

25
00:01:30,540 --> 00:01:34,980
between one network
and the other.

26
00:01:34,980 --> 00:01:38,140
The second main
role of a router,

27
00:01:38,140 --> 00:01:41,120
and they're
intertwined, is really

28
00:01:41,120 --> 00:01:45,200
about forwarding traffic
to some destination.

29
00:01:45,200 --> 00:01:49,740
This is where the
Internet comes in,

30
00:01:49,740 --> 00:01:51,840
and this is the
interconnection of a bunch

31
00:01:51,840 --> 00:01:54,460
of networks that all work

32
00:01:54,460 --> 00:01:55,840
together to allow for

33
00:01:55,840 --> 00:02:01,585
this global reachability
of any given traffic.

34
00:02:01,585 --> 00:02:04,890
That's the main
roles of a router.

35
00:02:04,890 --> 00:02:07,470
A lot of times you

36
00:02:07,470 --> 00:02:10,530
hear these two words,
forwarding and routing,

37
00:02:10,530 --> 00:02:12,350
and a lot of times you hear

38
00:02:12,350 --> 00:02:18,290
them spoken as if they're
the same word or same thing.

39
00:02:18,290 --> 00:02:20,970
They're actually two
different things.

40
00:02:20,970 --> 00:02:23,530
Forwarding really refers to

41
00:02:23,530 --> 00:02:25,610
the data plane of the network.

42
00:02:25,610 --> 00:02:28,470
This is the thing that directs

43
00:02:28,470 --> 00:02:32,775
a data packet to some
output port or link,

44
00:02:32,775 --> 00:02:36,880
and this is the thing that
uses a forwarding table.

45
00:02:36,880 --> 00:02:41,115
The routing, which is
the control plane,

46
00:02:41,115 --> 00:02:45,870
this is the coordination
between different routers.

47
00:02:45,870 --> 00:02:49,050
This is the thing that
computes the paths by

48
00:02:49,050 --> 00:02:52,605
coordinating with all
of its neighbors,

49
00:02:52,605 --> 00:02:56,810
and its job is to create
the forwarding table.

50
00:02:56,810 --> 00:03:00,410
All the routers have
both the data plane,

51
00:03:00,410 --> 00:03:03,530
which is the actual
traffic handling,

52
00:03:03,530 --> 00:03:06,270
and a control plane which is

53
00:03:06,270 --> 00:03:10,740
the coordination
with the neighbors.

54
00:03:11,070 --> 00:03:15,530
We're going to look
at packet forwarding.

55
00:03:15,530 --> 00:03:18,750
How this all works,

56
00:03:18,750 --> 00:03:21,990
like we said this control
plane and showed in

57
00:03:21,990 --> 00:03:25,070
this diagram as a
route processor,

58
00:03:25,070 --> 00:03:27,010
which is software running

59
00:03:27,010 --> 00:03:29,585
on a general-purpose processor,

60
00:03:29,585 --> 00:03:32,920
doing stuff to calculate
the forwarding table.

61
00:03:32,920 --> 00:03:34,750
In the data plane,

62
00:03:34,750 --> 00:03:38,170
the life of a packet would
typically be something

63
00:03:38,170 --> 00:03:39,970
like the packet is

64
00:03:39,970 --> 00:03:43,320
received at the ingress
of a line card.

65
00:03:43,320 --> 00:03:48,070
You could think of a line
card as very similar to

66
00:03:48,070 --> 00:03:50,010
a network interface card that

67
00:03:50,010 --> 00:03:52,870
has where the cables
are plugged into it,

68
00:03:52,870 --> 00:03:55,015
has some functionality on it.

69
00:03:55,015 --> 00:03:57,285
On that line card,

70
00:03:57,285 --> 00:04:01,480
we would do look-up
of the destination in

71
00:04:01,480 --> 00:04:03,600
a forwarding table
to determine where

72
00:04:03,600 --> 00:04:06,360
the output port would be,

73
00:04:06,360 --> 00:04:08,900
which direction to forward it,

74
00:04:08,900 --> 00:04:10,760
send the packet over

75
00:04:10,760 --> 00:04:14,470
some switching fabric
to an output port,

76
00:04:14,470 --> 00:04:18,300
and then the line card
then transmits the packet.

77
00:04:18,300 --> 00:04:20,560
We'll dive in a little bit what

78
00:04:20,560 --> 00:04:23,100
that switching
fabric looks like.

79
00:04:23,100 --> 00:04:25,940
We're going to look
at these two parts.

80
00:04:25,940 --> 00:04:28,760
We're going to look at the
processing that happens,

81
00:04:28,760 --> 00:04:33,080
as well as what does that
switch fabric look like.

82
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:37,320
The processing here
we're referring to

83
00:04:37,320 --> 00:04:41,680
what is happening inside
of these line cards.

84
00:04:41,740 --> 00:04:46,540
Shown here is a generic
router architecture

85
00:04:46,540 --> 00:04:49,515
for those line cards.

86
00:04:49,515 --> 00:04:52,695
Highly simplified,
made high-level,

87
00:04:52,695 --> 00:04:56,260
but it shows the key
components of it.

88
00:04:56,990 --> 00:05:00,090
We have a packet that would

89
00:05:00,090 --> 00:05:04,690
be received shown on
the left hand side.

90
00:05:04,960 --> 00:05:08,630
The main processing that
happens is you're looking up

91
00:05:08,630 --> 00:05:13,400
the IP address inside
of a forwarding table,

92
00:05:13,400 --> 00:05:16,910
and then you do some
updates to the header.

93
00:05:16,910 --> 00:05:20,840
For example, we talked about
the time to leave field,

94
00:05:20,840 --> 00:05:22,100
so that would be something like

95
00:05:22,100 --> 00:05:24,890
decermenting the
time to leave field.

96
00:05:24,890 --> 00:05:27,980
Then now that we know
where to send it,

97
00:05:27,980 --> 00:05:31,610
we que the packet up
inside of a buffer,

98
00:05:31,610 --> 00:05:34,175
and then when it's
ready to be sent,

99
00:05:34,175 --> 00:05:35,960
it can be sent on its way.

100
00:05:35,960 --> 00:05:38,720
The key challenge where a lot of

101
00:05:38,720 --> 00:05:41,270
the research over the years came

102
00:05:41,270 --> 00:05:43,820
in was on how to do a

103
00:05:43,820 --> 00:05:47,400
look up of an IP
address really fast.

104
00:05:49,390 --> 00:05:53,045
This is actually a
challenge and interesting.

105
00:05:53,045 --> 00:05:56,135
Let's compare it
first to Ethernet.

106
00:05:56,135 --> 00:05:58,280
Ethernet we saw
that you're doing

107
00:05:58,280 --> 00:06:01,670
this match on this
destination Mac address.

108
00:06:01,670 --> 00:06:06,740
The addresses are structured
around the vendors,

109
00:06:06,740 --> 00:06:11,030
we said last time that
it's this flat address,

110
00:06:11,030 --> 00:06:15,575
and it really has no relation to

111
00:06:15,575 --> 00:06:18,200
anything structure related to

112
00:06:18,200 --> 00:06:20,330
how to forward it
in the network.

113
00:06:20,330 --> 00:06:22,220
Because you can have
devices from a lot of

114
00:06:22,220 --> 00:06:25,115
different vendors and
things like that.

115
00:06:25,115 --> 00:06:31,680
Lookup in the Mac Address
tables are exact matches.

116
00:06:32,680 --> 00:06:37,790
Now let's compare that in IP,

117
00:06:37,790 --> 00:06:41,795
what we're actually doing is
matching on an IP prefix.

118
00:06:41,795 --> 00:06:43,760
We talked about how you get

119
00:06:43,760 --> 00:06:48,620
that scalability
in the Internet as

120
00:06:48,620 --> 00:06:52,325
a whole is by being
able to group

121
00:06:52,325 --> 00:06:57,440
a bunch of addresses together
into these prefixes.

122
00:06:57,440 --> 00:07:00,350
The challenge here
is that we may

123
00:07:00,350 --> 00:07:03,950
have prefixes that overlap.

124
00:07:03,950 --> 00:07:07,640
Shown here is an example
where we have two subnets,

125
00:07:07,640 --> 00:07:12,560
one is 11.11.0.0/16.

126
00:07:12,560 --> 00:07:18,035
Remember that means the
upper 16 bits or where

127
00:07:18,035 --> 00:07:22,985
each decimal in the dotted
decimal, there's four of them.

128
00:07:22,985 --> 00:07:25,850
Each of those represents
eight bits of

129
00:07:25,850 --> 00:07:31,700
uppermost decimals,
points the 11.11.

130
00:07:31,700 --> 00:07:35,150
Anything there, it covers that.

131
00:07:35,150 --> 00:07:36,590
But then we have down here

132
00:07:36,590 --> 00:07:40,010
a different subnet that's
a little more specific,

133
00:07:40,010 --> 00:07:46,140
it's the 11.11.11.0/24.

134
00:07:46,140 --> 00:07:51,320
We may have two
different IP addresses

135
00:07:51,320 --> 00:07:56,135
or table entries for
each of those prefix.

136
00:07:56,135 --> 00:08:00,950
Maybe there's in one
case go to the left,

137
00:08:00,950 --> 00:08:03,665
and in other case
you go to the right,

138
00:08:03,665 --> 00:08:07,560
or in this case
port 1 and port 2.

139
00:08:08,740 --> 00:08:14,015
Now if we get this
destination 11.11.11.11,

140
00:08:14,015 --> 00:08:17,120
it's actually covered by both.

141
00:08:17,120 --> 00:08:21,245
Then the question is which
should we send it to?

142
00:08:21,245 --> 00:08:23,900
This is where the concept of

143
00:08:23,900 --> 00:08:27,590
longest prefix match comes in.

144
00:08:27,590 --> 00:08:32,480
The job of the Lookup is to find

145
00:08:32,480 --> 00:08:37,835
the most specific prefix that
matches the destination.

146
00:08:37,835 --> 00:08:41,570
How a situation like this
might have occurred is, sure,

147
00:08:41,570 --> 00:08:45,095
there might be one
organization that has

148
00:08:45,095 --> 00:08:49,895
ownership over 11.11.0.0/16,

149
00:08:49,895 --> 00:08:53,810
but then they carved
out a portion

150
00:08:53,810 --> 00:08:57,770
of it for one
department or whatever,

151
00:08:57,770 --> 00:08:59,690
and that's actually in
a different location,

152
00:08:59,690 --> 00:09:01,235
has its own router.

153
00:09:01,235 --> 00:09:08,840
Now basically for anything
this subnet should go to here,

154
00:09:08,840 --> 00:09:13,895
and then everything else
would go to the other router.

155
00:09:13,895 --> 00:09:16,370
That's a common
situation that occurs.

156
00:09:16,370 --> 00:09:20,285
It occurs through the
aggregation process.

157
00:09:20,285 --> 00:09:24,600
A bunch of different
things that can occur.

158
00:09:24,700 --> 00:09:27,365
Here we want to find

159
00:09:27,365 --> 00:09:33,420
the most specific prefix that
matches the destination.

160
00:09:33,480 --> 00:09:36,970
To visualize this, we can view

161
00:09:36,970 --> 00:09:42,950
the entire IPV force address
space as a line right here,

162
00:09:42,950 --> 00:09:46,955
from 0-232 - 1.

163
00:09:46,955 --> 00:09:49,790
We have 32 bits,
so this would be

164
00:09:49,790 --> 00:09:51,800
all zeros and this would

165
00:09:51,800 --> 00:09:54,350
be all ones on the
right hand side.

166
00:09:54,350 --> 00:09:57,680
Now, any of our prefixes covers

167
00:09:57,680 --> 00:10:02,585
some portion of
this address space,

168
00:10:02,585 --> 00:10:07,610
with longer prefixes covering

169
00:10:07,610 --> 00:10:09,890
a smaller amount of
the address space,

170
00:10:09,890 --> 00:10:12,095
because they're more specific.

171
00:10:12,095 --> 00:10:17,770
The shorter prefixes,
like a /8 up at the top,

172
00:10:17,770 --> 00:10:22,155
covers a much wider
range of address spaces.

173
00:10:22,155 --> 00:10:24,830
Then any given address
that we're given,

174
00:10:24,830 --> 00:10:29,385
so for example, 11.5.50.50.

175
00:10:29,385 --> 00:10:32,915
That's a single point
in this address space,

176
00:10:32,915 --> 00:10:36,530
and we can view it as
looking as this line of

177
00:10:36,530 --> 00:10:40,890
which is the first
prefix that it matches.

178
00:10:40,890 --> 00:10:44,930
In this case it's 11.5.0.0/16.

179
00:10:44,930 --> 00:10:50,395
Another example 11.5.200.50.

180
00:10:50,395 --> 00:10:51,880
Similar exercise,

181
00:10:51,880 --> 00:10:55,825
and it actually hit right here,

182
00:10:55,825 --> 00:10:59,030
and this is looking
at a slash 24.

183
00:10:59,190 --> 00:11:03,910
That's one way to visualize it.

184
00:11:03,910 --> 00:11:08,020
But then another way to
think about it is how might

185
00:11:08,020 --> 00:11:09,730
this actually be
implemented if you were

186
00:11:09,730 --> 00:11:12,740
to implement it in code?

187
00:11:13,410 --> 00:11:16,735
Here's an example of
an address lookup

188
00:11:16,735 --> 00:11:21,160
using a try data structure.

189
00:11:21,160 --> 00:11:25,460
A try is just a type
of a search tree.

190
00:11:26,610 --> 00:11:29,785
When we start filling
out this table,

191
00:11:29,785 --> 00:11:33,970
what we do is each node can have

192
00:11:33,970 --> 00:11:42,235
a child node that if the next
bit in the value is zero,

193
00:11:42,235 --> 00:11:44,005
you would go to the left.

194
00:11:44,005 --> 00:11:45,805
If the next bit is a one,

195
00:11:45,805 --> 00:11:47,215
you go to the right.

196
00:11:47,215 --> 00:11:51,040
Then if there's a table
entry that actually

197
00:11:51,040 --> 00:11:56,125
matches at that particular
node in the tree,

198
00:11:56,125 --> 00:11:59,830
you would put the
prefix match there.

199
00:11:59,830 --> 00:12:05,455
For example, 100 starting
at the root of the tree,

200
00:12:05,455 --> 00:12:07,390
we would go one,

201
00:12:07,390 --> 00:12:10,570
go to the right child.

202
00:12:10,570 --> 00:12:14,470
Then we're here.
The next is zero,

203
00:12:14,470 --> 00:12:15,775
so you go to the left child,

204
00:12:15,775 --> 00:12:19,525
and then the next value is
zero and go to the left child.

205
00:12:19,525 --> 00:12:22,630
Since that's the entirety
of the prefix length,

206
00:12:22,630 --> 00:12:25,660
we would put that P1

207
00:12:25,660 --> 00:12:29,770
is right there in that entry
right there, in that node.

208
00:12:29,770 --> 00:12:34,105
Then we would go and
add 11 for prefix 2.

209
00:12:34,105 --> 00:12:38,275
One gets us to the right child,

210
00:12:38,275 --> 00:12:42,970
and then go one again
to the right child,

211
00:12:42,970 --> 00:12:45,880
and we put the prefix 2 there.

212
00:12:45,880 --> 00:12:48,130
We could just build out
this tree whenever you're

213
00:12:48,130 --> 00:12:52,075
adding and removing prefixes.

214
00:12:52,075 --> 00:12:55,600
Whenever the forwarding
table is updated,

215
00:12:55,600 --> 00:13:00,835
you could just keep
manipulating the tree.

216
00:13:00,835 --> 00:13:03,100
Now to perform the lookup,

217
00:13:03,100 --> 00:13:06,370
you would basically
just walk the tree.

218
00:13:06,370 --> 00:13:10,630
Here's the IP address
and it's shortened,

219
00:13:10,630 --> 00:13:15,515
just for illustration,
we would walk this tree.

220
00:13:15,515 --> 00:13:17,520
We would look at
the first bit is a

221
00:13:17,520 --> 00:13:18,975
one. We would go right.

222
00:13:18,975 --> 00:13:21,515
The next bit is one,
we would go right.

223
00:13:21,515 --> 00:13:23,920
Now we hit a prefix,

224
00:13:23,920 --> 00:13:26,140
but there may be
a longer prefix.

225
00:13:26,140 --> 00:13:28,855
We would keep
traversing the tree.

226
00:13:28,855 --> 00:13:31,870
Then the next bit is zero.

227
00:13:31,870 --> 00:13:33,955
Great. There actually is a child

228
00:13:33,955 --> 00:13:36,265
that's zero so we need
to traverse that.

229
00:13:36,265 --> 00:13:38,860
Then the next bit is zero.

230
00:13:38,860 --> 00:13:40,345
Since there's no child,

231
00:13:40,345 --> 00:13:43,195
we know that we hit the end,

232
00:13:43,195 --> 00:13:46,780
and whatever the
last prefix node

233
00:13:46,780 --> 00:13:49,345
that matched was
the longest prefix,

234
00:13:49,345 --> 00:13:51,770
and in this case, it's P2.

235
00:13:52,740 --> 00:13:58,615
That's a quick overview of
the design of a line card.

236
00:13:58,615 --> 00:14:01,630
There's a lot of interesting
aspects that you can look

237
00:14:01,630 --> 00:14:04,870
into and around the
queuing aspect of that.

238
00:14:04,870 --> 00:14:08,860
But we'll save that
for a different time.

239
00:14:08,860 --> 00:14:12,685
The next part is to look
at this switch fabric.

240
00:14:12,685 --> 00:14:15,200
What does that
actually look like?

241
00:14:15,630 --> 00:14:20,890
Here, it's useful to think
about how these historically

242
00:14:20,890 --> 00:14:23,290
evolve so that you
can appreciate

243
00:14:23,290 --> 00:14:26,215
the innovation at
each generation.

244
00:14:26,215 --> 00:14:28,690
Starting off in the
first generation,

245
00:14:28,690 --> 00:14:31,660
it was really
switching via memory.

246
00:14:31,660 --> 00:14:35,830
The interface would get the
packet off of the wire.

247
00:14:35,830 --> 00:14:39,940
It would transfer that from
the interface into memory.

248
00:14:39,940 --> 00:14:42,280
The processor would determine

249
00:14:42,280 --> 00:14:44,320
the next top it would do that,

250
00:14:44,320 --> 00:14:46,255
look up in the forwarding table,

251
00:14:46,255 --> 00:14:48,925
and then transfer the
packet from memory

252
00:14:48,925 --> 00:14:52,915
to the appropriate
output interface.

253
00:14:52,915 --> 00:14:55,420
Now you can guess the limitation

254
00:14:55,420 --> 00:14:58,570
here is the CPU processing.

255
00:14:58,570 --> 00:15:02,215
It's limited in the amount
of parallelism it can have.

256
00:15:02,215 --> 00:15:09,080
It's also extra transfers
and things like that.

257
00:15:09,420 --> 00:15:14,710
The next generation looked
